<chapter xml:id="ch-project-overview">
  <title>Project Overview</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      This project develops a method and computational workflow for analyzing the
      mathematical justification visible in short student responses. The responses
      come from mathematical questions embedded in interactive textbooks and are
      interpreted together with the exact question, the information supplied to the
      student, and the mathematical task the student was asked to complete.
    </p>

    <p>
      The central analytic object is an <term>episode of justification</term>: a local
      reasoning move in which a student cites a mathematical feature or condition and
      uses it to support a judgment. The project represents these episodes in a form
      that can be examined by human researchers and processed by a computational tool.
      The full definition appears in <xref ref="ch-episode-model" text="title"/>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The tool assists researchers by organizing responses, screening for codable
      justification, proposing episodes, comparing those episodes with a
      question-specific codebook, and routing uncertain cases for review. It does not
      replace mathematical or interpretive judgment. Human researchers define the
      analytic rules, review the tool's proposals, and remain responsible for the
      resulting research claims.
    </p>

    <p>
      The project therefore joins three related efforts:
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            develop a defensible method for describing local justification in short
            responses;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            implement that method as an auditable, human-in-the-loop computational
            workflow; and
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            evaluate whether the workflow improves or clarifies the reliability,
            efficiency, and transparency of response coding.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-project-problem">
    <title>The Analytic Problem</title>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-project-data">
      <title>What the Project Studies</title>

      <p>
        The basic unit of data is a student's written response to a specific
        mathematical question. A response cannot be interpreted responsibly in
        isolation. Its meaning depends on the surrounding problem context, including:
        <ul>
          <li>the exact question the student answered;</li>
          <li>the mathematical information and representations supplied;</li>
          <li>the conclusion, explanation, or computation requested; and</li>
          <li>
            the definitions, results, and forms of reasoning available in the course.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Most examples in this guide come from short-answer questions in linear
        algebra. The same analytic process can be applied to other mathematical
        topics, but each new question requires its own mathematical reference and
        question-specific coding information.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-project-unit">
      <title>Unit of Analysis</title>

      <p>
        The project focuses on local pieces of justification rather than treating an
        entire response as single entity. A response may contain no justification, 
        one episode, or several related episodes.
      </p>

      <p>
        Informally, an episode records three things:
        <ul>
          <li>the mathematical feature or condition the student cites;</li>
          <li>the inference connecting that feature to a conclusion; and</li>
          <li>the judgment the student draws.</li>
        </ul>
        Separating these components allows researchers to distinguish, for example, a
        false observation from an invalid inference or a sound local argument from an
        incomplete answer.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-project-boundaries">
      <title>What the Project Does Not Infer</title>

      <p>
        A written response provides evidence about the reasoning visible in that
        response. It does not provide direct access to everything the student knew,
        believed, considered, or did before writing.
      </p>

      <p>
        The project is motivated by the cK&#162; framework, especially its treatment
        of control structures, but it does not claim that a single short response
        reveals a complete student conception. The project may report that:
        <ul>
          <li>a response contains a particular episode of justification;</li>
          <li>a student appears to use a stated mathematical criterion; or</li>
          <li>a response provides evidence related to a control structure.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-project-goals">
    <title>Project Goals</title>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-project-analytic-goal">
      <title>Analytic Goal</title>

      <p>
        The analytic goal is to develop a clear and consistent method for describing
        the justification present in short mathematical responses. The method should:
        <ul>
          <li>preserve the student's stated evidence and conclusions;</li>
          <li>distinguish observations, inferences, and judgments;</li>
          <li>represent incomplete and ambiguous reasoning without repairing it;</li>
          <li>allow several reasoning moves to be recorded separately; and</li>
          <li>
            support comparison across coders, questions, topics, and analytic tools.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        The Episode Model provides this common representation. A question-specific
        codebook then connects extracted episodes to broader control-structure codes.
        The codebook and question-level analytic frame are introduced in
        <xref ref="ch-codebook" text="title"/>.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-project-computational-goal">
      <title>Computational Goal</title>

      <p>
        The computational goal is to support the analysis of large collections of
        responses without hiding the decisions that produce a code. The pipeline is
        designed to:
        <ul>
          <li>standardize responses while preserving their source information;</li>
          <li>screen for responses containing analyzable justification;</li>
          <li>propose one or more episodes from the response text;</li>
          <li>compare those episodes with the current question-specific codebook;</li>
          <li>record zero, one, or several candidate codes;</li>
          <li>
            flag incomplete, ambiguous, contradictory, or unfamiliar reasoning; and
          </li>
          <li>route each response to an appropriate form of human review.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Each stage records its output and rationale instead of retaining only a final
        label. This staged design allows a reviewer to determine whether a disagreement
        arose during screening, episode extraction, code matching, correctness
        assessment, or disposition. The pipeline is described in
        <xref ref="ch-pipeline-overview" text="title"/>.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-project-research-goal">
      <title>Research Goal</title>

      <p>
        The research goal is to determine whether the analytic method and supporting
        pipeline improve or clarify the coding process. The project does not assume
        that automation is beneficial merely because it produces codes quickly.
        Instead, it asks whether the workflow produces measurable gains in:
        <ul>
          <li>agreement with independently developed human coding;</li>
          <li>consistency across repeated analyses;</li>
          <li>efficiency and organization of researcher labor;</li>
          <li>transparency of intermediate decisions; and</li>
          <li>
            diagnosis of disagreements, codebook gaps, and recurring failure modes.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        A working software pipeline is therefore not the final research contribution.
        The contribution requires an empirical comparison with human coding and a
        careful analysis of errors, overrides, ambiguous cases, and codebook revisions.
        The proposed evaluation appears in
        <xref ref="ch-study-design" text="title"/>.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

