  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-project-questions">
    <title>Research Questions</title>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-project-reasoning-questions">
      <title>Questions About Student Justification</title>

      <p>
        The substantive analysis asks questions such as:
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              What mathematical criteria do students cite in short written
              justifications?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              What judgments do students draw from those criteria, and what
              inferential links connect them?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Which forms of control recur across questions, topics, or student
              groups?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              When do students cite a true criterion but use it through an invalid or
              incomplete inference?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              When does a response contain sound local reasoning but fail to address
              the complete goal of the question?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-project-method-questions">
      <title>Questions About the Analytic Method</title>

      <p>
        The methods study asks questions such as:
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              How consistently can human researchers identify and code episodes under
              the current framework and protocol?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Which screening, extraction, and matching decisions can a computational
              tool reproduce reliably, and which still require substantial human
              interpretation?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              How closely do deterministic and language-model-assisted workflows agree
              with independently developed human coding?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              How does the pipeline change the amount and distribution of researcher
              labor?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              What do disagreements among coders, tools, and codebook versions reveal
              about the strengths and weaknesses of the analytic method?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </section>

  <conclusion>
    <p>
      In summary, the Episode Model supplies the analytic unit, the pipeline supplies
      an auditable human-in-the-loop workflow, and the empirical study evaluates
      whether that workflow improves or clarifies the analysis of mathematical
      justification in short student responses.
    </p>
  </conclusion>
</chapter>
